# Geodesic thorn testsuite configuration.
#
# Nine tests.  Every one runs on a 50^3 KerrSchild grid over
# [-25,25]^3 (dx = 1, Driver::ghost_size = 4, Carpet excision of the
# r < 4 sphere via Geodesic::excised_radius) for 32 Cactus steps of
# dt = 0.1 with Geodesic::step = 100, i.e. the particle coordinate time
# advances 3200 M while the grid advances 3.2 M.  One test adds a single
# CarpetRegrid2 box.  Each test dumps the full particle state
# (t x y z u^t u^x u^y u^z tau cctk_time) once per Cactus step, 32-33
# rows, and the driver compares it row by row against the reference in
# the matching directory.
#
# What is covered:
#
#   circ_orbit           Exact = yes: the analytic Kerr-Schild metric and
#                        Christoffel symbols.  One particle on the
#                        prograde equatorial circular orbit r = 11.989
#                        (M = 2, a = 0.5).  Pins the ODE integrator, the
#                        u^mu renormalisation and the dump format.
#
#   circ_orbit_conserved the same run through util/check_conserved, which
#                        prints the Kerr constants of motion (E, L_z) for
#                        every row and compares those two columns; a broken
#                        integrator shows up as a drift of E / L_z.
#
#   circ_orbit_mpi       the same run on 2 MPI ranks.  In Exact mode the
#                        grid safe-region checks are skipped (each rank
#                        owns only part of the grid), so the dump must be
#                        bit-identical to the single-rank reference.  This
#                        is the rank-count independence test.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid      Exact = no: the metric and the Christoffel
#                        symbols come from the ADMBase grid (KerrSchild
#                        initial data in 3+1 form) through the default
#                        local27 sampler -- 5x5x5 node fit plus 6th-order
#                        nodal FD.  Carpet::init_fill_timelevels = yes
#                        makes the stationary-metric detection engage
#                        (d_t g = 0), as for the analytic metric.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid_l27  same, metric_interp = "l27": 3x3x3 node fit of
#                        metric and Christoffels, current time level only.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid_aei  same, metric_interp = "aei": AEILocalInterp's
#                        pointwise 4th-order Lagrange interpolant.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid_carpet
#                        same, metric_interp = "carpet": the same
#                        4th-order interpolant reached through
#                        LocalInterp instead of AEILocalInterp, i.e. the
#                        data-acquisition path rather than the stencil.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid_2p   the circ_orbit_grid run on 2 MPI ranks, with its
#                        own reference: the multi-rank stencil path
#                        (Geodesic's own halo exchange, GEODESIC_HALO_W = 5)
#                        must be reproducible at fixed rank count.
#
#   circ_orbit_amr_small the same orbit on the 50^3 base grid plus one
#                        static CarpetRegrid2 box of half-width 16
#                        (dx = 0.5), on 2 MPI ranks: exercises the
#                        per-level grid acquire and pick_level.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION, and why the grid-sampler references are generated at
# the rank count the test runs at: with Exact = no the trajectory is NOT
# bit-identical across different rank counts.  The grid data itself is
# bit-identical (checked by dumping ADMBase::metric with
# CarpetIOASCII::compact_format = yes, output_ghost_points = no), but the
# chunk boundaries follow the decomposition, so a particle is handed to a
# new owner at a different point along its path and the floating-point
# summation order changes: circ_orbit_grid_2p differs from the
# single-rank circ_orbit_grid by 9.1e-3 in x and 1.13e-2 in y at
# t = 3200.  Two rank-dependent mechanisms behind the earlier, much
# larger disagreement are now fixed: the halo exchange covers edges and
# corners as well as faces (a node Christoffel whose finite-difference
# window touched an unfilled edge cell used to fall back silently to the
# analytic assembly), and the chunk-boundary constraint is applied even
# to a face the particle sits exactly on (it used to be skipped, letting
# the particle cross the neighbouring subdomain in one step and be
# dropped).  Exact = yes is rank-count independent (circ_orbit_mpi).
# See doc/documentation.tex, "Known limitations".
#
# The heavy runs (100- and 200-particle rings, 3-level AMR, the
# TwoPunctures BBH sets, the conv100 sampler comparisons) are in
# test/extra/.  The Cactus testsuite driver globs test/*.par only, so
# they are not run automatically; test/extra/README explains how to run
# them as a local regression set.  They are too big for the testsuite:
# the review criteria are under 10 s per test and under 2 min for the
# whole component, and circ_orbit_amr alone takes ~20 min on one core.

EXTENSIONS txt

TEST circ_orbit
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 1
}

TEST circ_orbit_conserved
{
  POSTPROC check_conserved
  RELTOL 1e-8
  NPROCS 1
}

TEST circ_orbit_mpi
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 1
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid_l27
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid_aei
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid_carpet
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid_2p
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_amr_small
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}
